# What a k3s node needs from the distribution. k3s itself is ONE static binary (/usr/bin/k3s,
# fetched and checksum-verified by exec.config) that carries containerd, runc, the CNI plugins,
# flannel, kube-proxy and kubectl/crictl/ctr. The host still provides:
#
# the packet filter k3s drives. k3s prefers the host's iptables when there is one; Garden Linux's
# default alternative is iptables-nft (1.8.11), which k3s and kube-proxy support. The gardener element
# switches to iptables-legacy for Cilium; k3s' flannel has no such need, so the default stays.
iptables
conntrack
# (ipset is not in the Garden Linux repository; k3s carries its own in the binary's aux tools, which
# is what kube-router's network policy controller uses when the host has none.)
ethtool
socat
# containerd refuses to start a container when AppArmor is enabled in the kernel and the parser is
# missing ("apparmor_parser: not found"); the LSM is chosen explicitly in cmdline.d/90-lsm.cfg.
apparmor
# exec.config downloads the k3s release with it, and it is the one tool every debugging session
# reaches for first. ca-certificates for the TLS in both cases.
curl
ca-certificates
jq
#
# LOCAL VOLUMES — the same package set features/robot ships for the dedicated pool, and for the same
# reasons (read that file's comments): the CSI (OpenEBS LocalPV-LVM) carves logical volumes out of vg0,
# thin_check guards the thin pool's activation, dmeventd makes thin_pool_autoextend real, mkfs.xfs
# must exist ON THE NODE for an xfs class.
lvm2
thin-provisioning-tools
dmeventd
xfsprogs
